308
00:11:58,079 --> 00:12:03,640
signals. And mat lab makes it easy to

309
00:12:00,640 --> 00:12:03,640
visualize.

310
00:12:05,360 --> 00:12:08,800
Now let's think about circular

311
00:12:06,720 --> 00:12:10,880
convolution.

312
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:12,639
Earlier with linear convolution, we had

313
00:12:10,880 --> 00:12:14,959
just one pair of signals sliding past

314
00:12:12,639 --> 00:12:18,240
each other. In this hand analogy, that

315
00:12:14,959 --> 00:12:20,320
meant one top hand and one bottom hand.

316
00:12:18,240 --> 00:12:22,639
Here with circular convolution, the

317
00:12:20,320 --> 00:12:24,639
situation is different.

318
00:12:22,639 --> 00:12:26,880
The signal is treated as if it repeats

319
00:12:24,639 --> 00:12:29,360
periodically, just like in our earlier

320
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:31,680
example from Wikipedia. So instead of

321
00:12:29,360 --> 00:12:35,279
one top hand, we now have several copies

322
00:12:31,680 --> 00:12:37,360
in a row as shown here with three hands.

323
00:12:35,279 --> 00:12:39,360
When we slide the lower signal, we can't

324
00:12:37,360 --> 00:12:41,839
run out of data on either end because

325
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:43,680
the pattern simply wraps around. That

326
00:12:41,839 --> 00:12:46,720
means parts of the signal from the end

327
00:12:43,680 --> 00:12:48,560
overlap with parts from the beginning.

328
00:12:46,720 --> 00:12:50,399
In the picture, you can see that the

329
00:12:48,560 --> 00:12:52,000
fingers from the lower hands are

330
00:12:50,399 --> 00:12:54,240
overlapping with the fingers from the

331
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:56,480
upper hands. Not just in the middle, but

332
00:12:54,240 --> 00:12:58,240
also across the boundaries. This

333
00:12:56,480 --> 00:12:59,920
