387
00:15:06,560 --> 00:15:12,480
and then falls as the overlap decreases.

388
00:15:10,240 --> 00:15:15,279
If this feels familiar, it's because we

389
00:15:12,480 --> 00:15:17,440
used the same function conv earlier when

390
00:15:15,279 --> 00:15:19,760
convolving the RC circuits impulse

391
00:15:17,440 --> 00:15:22,320
response with the unitstep function in

392
00:15:19,760 --> 00:15:24,560
your homework. The process is exactly

393
00:15:22,320 --> 00:15:27,440
the same here. Only the input sequences

394
00:15:24,560 --> 00:15:29,600
are different.

395
00:15:27,440 --> 00:15:31,600
This example is a nice reminder that mat

396
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:34,000
lab makes it very easy to visualize

397
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:36,160
convolution and seeing the result in a

398
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:39,160
plot helps reinforce what's happening

399
00:15:36,160 --> 00:15:39,160
mathematically.

400
00:15:41,040 --> 00:15:44,720
Here's where we can clearly see the

401
00:15:42,800 --> 00:15:47,040
difference between linear convolution

402
00:15:44,720 --> 00:15:48,800
and circular convolution without zero

403
00:15:47,040 --> 00:15:51,040
padding.

404
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:53,279
In mat lab we can perform circular

405
00:15:51,040 --> 00:15:55,120
convolution using the forier transform

406
00:15:53,279 --> 00:15:57,120
approach.

407
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:59,680
First we take the FFT of our two

408
00:15:57,120 --> 00:16:02,320
sequences X and H. Then we multiply

409
00:15:59,680 --> 00:16:04,720
their spectra point by point. Finally we

410
00:16:02,320 --> 00:16:07,440
take the inverse FFT to bring the result

411
00:16:04,720 --> 00:16:09,360
back into the time domain.

412
00:16:07,440 --> 00:16:10,959
Mathematically, multiplication in the

413
00:16:09,360 --> 00:16:12,959
