558
00:21:48,720 --> 00:21:52,880
resolution is limited by the number of

559
00:21:50,480 --> 00:21:54,880
samples we have. The first frequency

560
00:21:52,880 --> 00:21:57,360
happens to align perfectly with a bin,

561
00:21:54,880 --> 00:21:59,360
so it looks sharp. The second one falls

562
00:21:57,360 --> 00:22:01,520
between bins, so its energy is spread

563
00:21:59,360 --> 00:22:03,440
out over multiple points in the plot,

564
00:22:01,520 --> 00:22:05,360
making it appear a bit blurry and less

565
00:22:03,440 --> 00:22:07,280
distinct.

566
00:22:05,360 --> 00:22:09,200
We'll see in the next slide how zero

567
00:22:07,280 --> 00:22:11,039
padding helps us refine the spectral

568
00:22:09,200 --> 00:22:15,000
bins and make that second frequency

569
00:22:11,039 --> 00:22:15,000
stand out much more clearly.

570
00:22:16,559 --> 00:22:20,159
Now, let's see what happens when we add

571
00:22:18,159 --> 00:22:22,000
zero padding before taking the forier

572
00:22:20,159 --> 00:22:24,240
transform.

573
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:26,960
This time when we call the FFT function,

574
00:22:24,240 --> 00:22:28,960
we specify a length of 2,00. That means

575
00:22:26,960 --> 00:22:31,200
we take our original signal and append

576
00:22:28,960 --> 00:22:34,559
enough zeros to make it 2,000 points

577
00:22:31,200 --> 00:22:36,480
long before computing the FFT.

578
00:22:34,559 --> 00:22:39,120
By doing this, we change the spacing

579
00:22:36,480 --> 00:22:40,960
between frequency bins.

580
00:22:39,120 --> 00:22:43,280
Previously, without padding, the bins

581
00:22:40,960 --> 00:22:46,960
were one hertz apart. Now, the bin

582
00:22:43,280 --> 00:22:50,159
spacing is FS / 2000, which equals 0.5

583
00:22:46,960 --> 00:22:52,240
Hz. This finer spacing allows us to more

584
00:22:50,159 --> 00:22:55,679
accurately pinpoint frequencies that

585
00:22:52,240 --> 00:22:57,520
fall between the old one herz bins.

586
00:22:55,679 --> 00:23:00,720
When we plot the result, we still see

587
00:22:57,520 --> 00:23:03,600
the sharp spike at 100 hertz. Also, we

588
00:23:00,720 --> 00:23:05,600
now see a very clear spike at 202.5

589
00:23:03,600 --> 00:23:07,679
hertz, the second frequency in our

590
00:23:05,600 --> 00:23:09,840
original signal.

591
00:23:07,679 --> 00:23:11,679
The reason is simple. With zero padding,

592
00:23:09,840 --> 00:23:13,840
we've increased the frequency resolution

593
00:23:11,679 --> 00:23:15,760
of the spectrum, making it possible to

594
00:23:13,840 --> 00:23:18,000
distinguish both components of our

595
00:23:15,760 --> 00:23:20,159
signal cleanly.

596
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:22,320
So zero padding doesn't create new

597
00:23:20,159 --> 00:23:24,559
information. It simply lets us view the

598
00:23:22,320 --> 00:23:26,400
spectrum at a finer scale, revealing

599
00:23:24,559 --> 00:23:30,679
details that would otherwise be hidden

600
00:23:26,400 --> 00:23:30,679
between coarse frequency bins.

601
00:23:32,159 --> 00:23:36,559
Let's look at a more complex example, a

602
00:23:34,480 --> 00:23:38,960
continuous signal that contains several

603
00:23:36,559 --> 00:23:40,960
frequency components.

604
00:23:38,960 --> 00:23:43,919
We start with the same two frequencies

605
00:23:40,960 --> 00:23:47,360
from our earlier example. A cosine wave

606
00:23:43,919 --> 00:23:49,120
at 100 htz and a sine wave at 202.5

607
00:23:47,360 --> 00:23:51,520
hertz.

608
00:23:49,120 --> 00:23:54,960
Then we add three more components. A

609
00:23:51,520 --> 00:24:00,720
cosine wave at 45 htz, a cosine wave at

610
00:23:54,960 --> 00:24:02,880
407 herz and a sine wave at 445.8 hertz.

611
00:24:00,720 --> 00:24:04,720
When we add all these together, we get a

612
00:24:02,880 --> 00:24:07,280
signal that looks quite messy in the

613
00:24:04,720 --> 00:24:09,440
time domain. As you can see in the plot,

614
00:24:07,280 --> 00:24:11,679
the rapid oscillations and varying

615
00:24:09,440 --> 00:24:14,240
amplitudes come from the interaction of

616
00:24:11,679 --> 00:24:16,720
all these frequencies.

617
00:24:14,240 --> 00:24:18,400
Looking at this time domain plot alone,

618
00:24:16,720 --> 00:24:20,240
it's not easy to tell exactly what

619
00:24:18,400 --> 00:24:22,400
frequencies are present or how strong

620
00:24:20,240 --> 00:24:24,880
they are. That's where the forier

621
00:24:22,400 --> 00:24:26,720
transform becomes incredibly valuable.

622
00:24:24,880 --> 00:24:28,960
It lets us switch to the frequency

623
00:24:26,720 --> 00:24:31,200
domain where each frequency component

624
00:24:28,960 --> 00:24:35,200
appears as a distinct peak making the

625
00:24:31,200 --> 00:24:37,360
signal's composition much clearer.

626
00:24:35,200 --> 00:24:39,279
In the next step, we'll apply the Forier

627
00:24:37,360 --> 00:24:41,120
transform to this signal and see how it

628
00:24:39,279 --> 00:24:45,559
reveals the underlying structure that's

629
00:24:41,120 --> 00:24:45,559
hidden in the time domain view.

630
00:24:46,960 --> 00:24:50,320
Here's the spectrum of our

631
00:24:48,159 --> 00:24:51,919
multifrequency signal without zero

632
00:24:50,320 --> 00:24:54,000
padding and without refining the

633
00:24:51,919 --> 00:24:56,000
frequency bins.

634
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:58,080
When we take the FFT and plot the

635
00:24:56,000 --> 00:25:00,159
magnitude against frequency in hertz,

636
00:24:58,080 --> 00:25:02,240
some frequencies stand out clearly while

637
00:25:00,159 --> 00:25:04,799
others do not.

638
00:25:02,240 --> 00:25:06,559
Look at 45 Hz. This is a clean discrete

639
00:25:04,799 --> 00:25:09,679
value and it aligns perfectly with an

640
00:25:06,559 --> 00:25:11,919
FFT bin. As a result, its spike reaches

641
00:25:09,679 --> 00:25:13,760
the maximum amplitude of one just as we

642
00:25:11,919 --> 00:25:16,640
would expect.

643
00:25:13,760 --> 00:25:19,919
But for the frequencies at 202.5 Hz and

644
00:25:16,640 --> 00:25:21,840
445.8 hertz, things are different.

645
00:25:19,919 --> 00:25:24,240
Because these frequencies do not align

646
00:25:21,840 --> 00:25:26,400
exactly with the FFT's default bin

647
00:25:24,240 --> 00:25:28,960
spacing, their energy is spread across

648
00:25:26,400 --> 00:25:31,120
multiple bins. This makes their peaks

649
00:25:28,960 --> 00:25:33,760
appear lower than one and less sharply

650
00:25:31,120 --> 00:25:36,080
defined.

651
00:25:33,760 --> 00:25:38,480
So without zero padding and therefore

652
00:25:36,080 --> 00:25:41,120
without finer bin spacing, our frequency

653
00:25:38,480 --> 00:25:43,200
resolution is limited. Frequencies that

654
00:25:41,120 --> 00:25:44,799
happen to fall exactly on a bin are well

655
00:25:43,200 --> 00:25:46,799
resolved, but others that fall in

656
00:25:44,799 --> 00:25:48,400
between bins are smeared out and not as

657
00:25:46,799 --> 00:25:50,159
clear.

658
00:25:48,400 --> 00:25:51,840
Next, we'll see how refining the bins

659
00:25:50,159 --> 00:25:53,600
through zero padding improves our

660
00:25:51,840 --> 00:25:57,159
ability to clearly detect those non-

661
00:25:53,600 --> 00:25:57,159
integer frequencies.

662
00:25:58,880 --> 00:26:03,440
Now let's refine our frequency bins by a

663
00:26:01,200 --> 00:26:05,840
factor of two. The same idea we used

664
00:26:03,440 --> 00:26:08,880
earlier when going from one herz bins to

665
00:26:05,840 --> 00:26:10,960
0.5 herz bins.

666
00:26:08,880 --> 00:26:13,440
We do this by setting a bin refine

667
00:26:10,960 --> 00:26:15,919
factor equal to two and then increasing

668
00:26:13,440 --> 00:26:18,000
the FFT length to be two times the

669
00:26:15,919 --> 00:26:19,679
number of samples in our original time

670
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:21,840
vector.

671
00:26:19,679 --> 00:26:23,840
In practice, this means appending enough

672
00:26:21,840 --> 00:26:26,799
zeros to double the length of the signal

673
00:26:23,840 --> 00:26:28,960
before computing the FFT.

674
00:26:26,799 --> 00:26:32,240
When we plot the result, the improvement

675
00:26:28,960 --> 00:26:34,799
is clear. The frequency at 202.5 hertz

676
00:26:32,240 --> 00:26:37,360
is now well resolved, showing a sharp

677
00:26:34,799 --> 00:26:39,520
and distinct peak.

678
00:26:37,360 --> 00:26:41,120
However, notice that the frequency at

679
00:26:39,520 --> 00:26:43,679
445.8

680
00:26:41,120 --> 00:26:46,480
hertz is still not well resolved. Even

681
00:26:43,679 --> 00:26:48,640
with a bin spacing of 0.5 hertz, this

682
00:26:46,480 --> 00:26:51,440
frequency does not fall exactly on one

683
00:26:48,640 --> 00:26:53,919
of the discrete FFT bins. So, its energy

684
00:26:51,440 --> 00:26:55,919
is still spread across multiple bins.

685
00:26:53,919 --> 00:26:57,440
The peak is visible but not as sharp or

686
00:26:55,919 --> 00:27:00,400
as tall as the perfectly aligned

687
00:26:57,440 --> 00:27:02,400
frequencies.

688
00:27:00,400 --> 00:27:04,720
The takeaway is that zero padding and

689
00:27:02,400 --> 00:27:06,400
bin refinement improve resolution but

690
00:27:04,720 --> 00:27:08,320
they cannot create a perfectly sharp

691
00:27:06,400 --> 00:27:12,679
spike unless the frequency exactly

692
00:27:08,320 --> 00:27:12,679
matches a discrete bin location.

693
00:27:14,240 --> 00:27:19,600
Now let's refine our frequency bins even

694
00:27:16,320 --> 00:27:21,840
further. This time by a factor of four.

695
00:27:19,600 --> 00:27:24,000
In mat lab that means setting the bin

696
00:27:21,840 --> 00:27:26,240
refine factor to four and increasing the

697
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:28,320
FFT length to four times the length of

698
00:27:26,240 --> 00:27:30,320
our original time vector. In other

699
00:27:28,320 --> 00:27:32,400
words, we append enough zeros so that

700
00:27:30,320 --> 00:27:34,960
the signal length is quadrupled before

701
00:27:32,400 --> 00:27:37,200
computing the FFT.

702
00:27:34,960 --> 00:27:40,559
By doing this, our frequency bin spacing

703
00:27:37,200 --> 00:27:42,559
changes from 1 herz to 0.2 hertz. This

704
00:27:40,559 --> 00:27:45,840
very fine resolution means that even

705
00:27:42,559 --> 00:27:48,000
frequencies like 445.8 8 hertz which

706
00:27:45,840 --> 00:27:50,080
were previously hard to resolve now

707
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:52,799
align closely with a bin and show a

708
00:27:50,080 --> 00:27:55,120
sharp well- definfined spike.

709
00:27:52,799 --> 00:27:58,240
When we plot the result, every frequency

710
00:27:55,120 --> 00:28:03,600
in our multicomponent signal 45 htz, 100

711
00:27:58,240 --> 00:28:05,600
htz, 202.5 htz, 407 herz, and 445.8 htz

712
00:28:03,600 --> 00:28:08,880
is clearly visible each with a maximum

713
00:28:05,600 --> 00:28:10,880
amplitude of one.

714
00:28:08,880 --> 00:28:13,039
This example confirms that refining the

715
00:28:10,880 --> 00:28:14,960
bin spacing through zero padding greatly

716
00:28:13,039 --> 00:28:17,200
improves our ability to detect non-

717
00:28:14,960 --> 00:28:19,440
integer frequencies. With enough

718
00:28:17,200 --> 00:28:21,600
refinement, even challenging frequencies

719
00:28:19,440 --> 00:28:23,760
can be resolved as sharply as those that

720
00:28:21,600 --> 00:28:26,760
naturally align with the original bin

721
00:28:23,760 --> 00:28:26,760
spacing.

722
00:28:28,399 --> 00:28:32,559
So far, we've looked at the forier

723
00:28:30,159 --> 00:28:35,360
transform in one dimension, signals that

724
00:28:32,559 --> 00:28:37,360
vary with time. But forier analysis is

725
00:28:35,360 --> 00:28:39,120
just as powerful in two dimensions. And

726
00:28:37,360 --> 00:28:40,960
that's exactly what we need for image

727
00:28:39,120 --> 00:28:42,960
processing.

728
00:28:40,960 --> 00:28:44,799
In the context of images, the two

729
00:28:42,960 --> 00:28:47,120
dimensions are the horizontal and

730
00:28:44,799 --> 00:28:50,000
vertical spatial coordinates rather than

731
00:28:47,120 --> 00:28:52,159
time. Just as a one-dimensional forier

732
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:54,399
transform tells us what frequencies are

733
00:28:52,159 --> 00:28:56,720
present in a signal, a two-dimensional

734
00:28:54,399 --> 00:29:00,960
forier transform tells us what spatial

735
00:28:56,720 --> 00:29:02,880
frequencies are present in an image.

736
00:29:00,960 --> 00:29:05,039
This is a critical tool in image

737
00:29:02,880 --> 00:29:07,440
analysis because many image processing

738
00:29:05,039 --> 00:29:10,000
tasks such as noise removal or edge

739
00:29:07,440 --> 00:29:13,039
enhancement are easier to understand and

740
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:15,679
perform in the frequency domain.

741
00:29:13,039 --> 00:29:17,679
In the next slides, we'll see how 2D4A

742
00:29:15,679 --> 00:29:19,840
transforms can be applied to filtering

743
00:29:17,679 --> 00:29:24,039
images. Starting with noise removal and

744
00:29:19,840 --> 00:29:24,039
then moving on to edge enhancement.

745
00:29:25,679 --> 00:29:30,399
Here we see the concept of the

746
00:29:27,200 --> 00:29:32,480
two-dimensional forier transform.

747
00:29:30,399 --> 00:29:35,200
The equations at the top describe two

748
00:29:32,480 --> 00:29:37,200
processes. The forward 2D forier

749
00:29:35,200 --> 00:29:39,120
transform which takes a spatial domain

750
00:29:37,200 --> 00:29:41,520
function, something that varies in the x

751
00:29:39,120 --> 00:29:43,120
and y directions and expresses it in

752
00:29:41,520 --> 00:29:45,039
terms of its spatial frequency

753
00:29:43,120 --> 00:29:47,360
components.

754
00:29:45,039 --> 00:29:49,760
The inverse 2D forier transform takes

755
00:29:47,360 --> 00:29:51,919
that frequency domain representation and

756
00:29:49,760 --> 00:29:54,640
reconstructs the original spatial domain

757
00:29:51,919 --> 00:29:56,480
function.

758
00:29:54,640 --> 00:29:58,399
In our example, the starting function is

759
00:29:56,480 --> 00:30:00,320
shown as a sideways rectangle in the

760
00:29:58,399 --> 00:30:02,720
spatial domain.

761
00:30:00,320 --> 00:30:04,559
When we perform the 2D forier transform,

762
00:30:02,720 --> 00:30:06,799
we obtain a frequency domain

763
00:30:04,559 --> 00:30:09,200
representation shown here in the rainbow

764
00:30:06,799 --> 00:30:11,440
colored plot. The colors represent the

765
00:30:09,200 --> 00:30:13,919
amplitude of different spatial frequency

766
00:30:11,440 --> 00:30:17,520
components and the values can be complex

767
00:30:13,919 --> 00:30:19,520
with both real and imaginary parts.

768
00:30:17,520 --> 00:30:22,000
Just as in the one-dimensional case, the

769
00:30:19,520 --> 00:30:24,640
forier transform lets us switch back and

770
00:30:22,000 --> 00:30:26,159
forth between two perspectives. In the

771
00:30:24,640 --> 00:30:28,320
spatial domain, we see shapes and

772
00:30:26,159 --> 00:30:30,240
patterns directly.

773
00:30:28,320 --> 00:30:32,320
In the frequency domain, we see how much

774
00:30:30,240 --> 00:30:35,200
of each spatial frequency is present in

775
00:30:32,320 --> 00:30:36,880
the image.

776
00:30:35,200 --> 00:30:38,640
Understanding this relationship is

777
00:30:36,880 --> 00:30:41,120
essential for image processing because

778
00:30:38,640 --> 00:30:43,200
many operations such as filtering, noise

779
00:30:41,120 --> 00:30:44,720
removal, and edge detection can be

780
00:30:43,200 --> 00:30:48,200
performed more effectively in the

781
00:30:44,720 --> 00:30:48,200
frequency domain.

782
00:30:49,760 --> 00:30:54,159
In image processing, noise can often

783
00:30:51,679 --> 00:30:56,240
hide important details.

784
00:30:54,159 --> 00:30:58,399
This is a common problem in real world

785
00:30:56,240 --> 00:31:00,720
applications, especially when images are

786
00:30:58,399 --> 00:31:03,360
captured in low light, transmitted over

787
00:31:00,720 --> 00:31:06,080
noisy channels or acquired using sensors

788
00:31:03,360 --> 00:31:07,840
in a challenging environment.

789
00:31:06,080 --> 00:31:10,159
Here we start with an image, the

790
00:31:07,840 --> 00:31:12,880
well-known Lena test image that has been

791
00:31:10,159 --> 00:31:14,640
corrupted by noise. In the top left, you

792
00:31:12,880 --> 00:31:16,399
can see the noisy image in the spatial

793
00:31:14,640 --> 00:31:18,559
domain.

794
00:31:16,399 --> 00:31:20,480
When we take the two-dimensional forier

795
00:31:18,559 --> 00:31:22,880
transform of this image shown in the

796
00:31:20,480 --> 00:31:25,279
bottom left, we see its frequency domain

797
00:31:22,880 --> 00:31:27,440
representation. The bright spot in the

798
00:31:25,279 --> 00:31:29,360
center corresponds to low frequency

799
00:31:27,440 --> 00:31:31,440
components which carry the main shapes

800
00:31:29,360 --> 00:31:33,039
and structures of the image. The

801
00:31:31,440 --> 00:31:35,039
scattered speckles throughout the

802
00:31:33,039 --> 00:31:36,799
spectrum represent higher frequency

803
00:31:35,039 --> 00:31:38,480
noise.

804
00:31:36,799 --> 00:31:40,480
If we want to reduce noise, we can

805
00:31:38,480 --> 00:31:42,480
selectively remove certain frequency

806
00:31:40,480 --> 00:31:44,240
components.

807
00:31:42,480 --> 00:31:46,159
One simple way is to set the outer

808
00:31:44,240 --> 00:31:48,000
regions of the spectrum to zero while

809
00:31:46,159 --> 00:31:50,320
keeping only the frequencies near the

810
00:31:48,000 --> 00:31:52,720
center. This is essentially a lowass

811
00:31:50,320 --> 00:31:54,399
filter in the frequency domain. In the

812
00:31:52,720 --> 00:31:57,200
bottom right, you can see this filtered

813
00:31:54,399 --> 00:31:59,279
spectrum.

814
00:31:57,200 --> 00:32:01,279
Finally, applying the inverse forier

815
00:31:59,279 --> 00:32:03,519
transform gives us the restored image in

816
00:32:01,279 --> 00:32:05,679
the top right. The noise is greatly

817
00:32:03,519 --> 00:32:08,000
reduced and the underlying features such

818
00:32:05,679 --> 00:32:09,760
as the woman's face and hat become much

819
00:32:08,000 --> 00:32:11,600
clearer.

820
00:32:09,760 --> 00:32:13,440
This kind of frequency domain noise

821
00:32:11,600 --> 00:32:15,279
suppression is a powerful technique in

822
00:32:13,440 --> 00:32:17,120
imaging, allowing us to recover

823
00:32:15,279 --> 00:32:20,360
important details that would otherwise

824
00:32:17,120 --> 00:32:20,360
be lost.

825
00:32:21,919 --> 00:32:26,399
We've just seen how Forier transforms

826
00:32:23,760 --> 00:32:27,919
can be used to remove noise.

827
00:32:26,399 --> 00:32:30,000
Now, let's look at two related

828
00:32:27,919 --> 00:32:32,159
techniques, lowass filtering and

829
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:34,080
highpass filtering, and how they affect

830
00:32:32,159 --> 00:32:35,679
an image.

831
00:32:34,080 --> 00:32:38,080
Starting with the original image of the

832
00:32:35,679 --> 00:32:40,080
building shown in the top left, we first

833
00:32:38,080 --> 00:32:42,399
take its two-dimensional forier

834
00:32:40,080 --> 00:32:44,159
transform. This gives us the frequency

835
00:32:42,399 --> 00:32:46,000
domain representation shown in the

836
00:32:44,159 --> 00:32:48,080
bottom left.

837
00:32:46,000 --> 00:32:51,840
If we want to blur the image or remove

838
00:32:48,080 --> 00:32:53,679
fine details, we apply a lowass filter

839
00:32:51,840 --> 00:32:55,279
in the frequency domain. This means

840
00:32:53,679 --> 00:32:56,799
keeping only the low frequency

841
00:32:55,279 --> 00:32:59,039
components near the center of the

842
00:32:56,799 --> 00:33:01,200
spectrum as shown in the middle bottom

843
00:32:59,039 --> 00:33:04,000
image and setting the high frequencies

844
00:33:01,200 --> 00:33:06,640
to zero. After applying the inverse

845
00:33:04,000 --> 00:33:08,880
furier transform, we get the middle top

846
00:33:06,640 --> 00:33:10,799
image. The overall structure is

847
00:33:08,880 --> 00:33:12,720
preserved, but the sharp edges are

848
00:33:10,799 --> 00:33:14,159
softened.

849
00:33:12,720 --> 00:33:16,320
On the other hand, if we want to

850
00:33:14,159 --> 00:33:18,960
highlight edges and fine details, we use

851
00:33:16,320 --> 00:33:20,640
a highpass filter.

852
00:33:18,960 --> 00:33:22,399
This is done by removing the low

853
00:33:20,640 --> 00:33:24,480
frequency center and keeping only the

854
00:33:22,399 --> 00:33:26,799
higher frequencies in the spectrum, as

855
00:33:24,480 --> 00:33:29,200
shown in the bottom right. The resulting

856
00:33:26,799 --> 00:33:33,039
image in the top right emphasizes edges

857
00:33:29,200 --> 00:33:35,039
and textures but loses smooth gradients.

858
00:33:33,039 --> 00:33:37,519
Lowass and highpass filters are

859
00:33:35,039 --> 00:33:39,200
fundamental tools in image processing.

860
00:33:37,519 --> 00:33:41,279
Whether we want to smooth an image,

861
00:33:39,200 --> 00:33:44,679
detect edges, or prepare data for

862
00:33:41,279 --> 00:33:44,679
further analysis.

863
00:33:46,480 --> 00:33:50,559
In image processing, we often use

864
00:33:48,480 --> 00:33:52,559
specialized filters to enhance certain

865
00:33:50,559 --> 00:33:54,720
features or to suppress unwanted

866
00:33:52,559 --> 00:33:56,480
information. Here are three common

867
00:33:54,720 --> 00:33:59,519
examples shown both in the spatial

868
00:33:56,480 --> 00:34:02,320
domain and in the frequency domain.

869
00:33:59,519 --> 00:34:04,320
First, the unsharp filter.

870
00:34:02,320 --> 00:34:06,880
Despite the name, this filter is used to

871
00:34:04,320 --> 00:34:08,800
sharpen images. It works by subtracting

872
00:34:06,880 --> 00:34:11,280
a blurred version of the image from the

873
00:34:08,800 --> 00:34:13,119
original, which enhances the edges. In

874
00:34:11,280 --> 00:34:15,200
the spatial domain, you can see the

875
00:34:13,119 --> 00:34:17,040
filter kernel, dark around the edges,

876
00:34:15,200 --> 00:34:21,359
light in the center, designed to

877
00:34:17,040 --> 00:34:23,679
emphasize changes in intensity.

878
00:34:21,359 --> 00:34:25,839
Next, the Gaussian filter. This is a

879
00:34:23,679 --> 00:34:27,919
smoothing filter which reduces noise in

880
00:34:25,839 --> 00:34:30,159
small details.

881
00:34:27,919 --> 00:34:32,720
In the spatial domain, it appears as a

882
00:34:30,159 --> 00:34:35,200
soft bell-shaped pattern, bright in the

883
00:34:32,720 --> 00:34:37,119
middle, fading smoothly outward. Its

884
00:34:35,200 --> 00:34:39,040
frequency domain representation shows

885
00:34:37,119 --> 00:34:42,320
that it preserves low frequencies while

886
00:34:39,040 --> 00:34:44,560
gradually suppressing high frequencies.

887
00:34:42,320 --> 00:34:46,399
Finally, the soil filter. This is an

888
00:34:44,560 --> 00:34:49,599
edge detection filter which highlights

889
00:34:46,399 --> 00:34:51,520
regions of rapid intensity change.

890
00:34:49,599 --> 00:34:53,440
In the spatial domain, you can see its

891
00:34:51,520 --> 00:34:55,679
distinctive pattern. one side light, the

892
00:34:53,440 --> 00:34:57,760
other dark, which responds strongly to

893
00:34:55,679 --> 00:35:00,240
vertical or horizontal edges depending

894
00:34:57,760 --> 00:35:02,079
on its orientation. In the frequency

895
00:35:00,240 --> 00:35:04,079
domain, its pattern reflects the fact

896
00:35:02,079 --> 00:35:05,920
that it suppresses low frequencies and

897
00:35:04,079 --> 00:35:08,920
emphasizes specific directional

898
00:35:05,920 --> 00:35:08,920
components.

899
00:35:08,960 --> 00:35:13,040
Mat Lab provides built-in functions for

900
00:35:11,200 --> 00:35:16,320
these filters, including F special for

901
00:35:13,040 --> 00:35:18,079
2D and F special 3 for 3D filtering, as

902
00:35:16,320 --> 00:35:20,560
well as options to design your own

903
00:35:18,079 --> 00:35:22,720
custom kernels. By choosing the right

904
00:35:20,560 --> 00:35:24,960
filter and domain of application,

905
00:35:22,720 --> 00:35:27,040
spatial or frequency, we can control

906
00:35:24,960 --> 00:35:30,760
exactly what features of an image are

907
00:35:27,040 --> 00:35:30,760
enhanced or suppressed.

908
00:35:32,400 --> 00:35:36,240
Here we have an example showing the

909
00:35:34,079 --> 00:35:37,920
effects of different image filters both

910
00:35:36,240 --> 00:35:40,079
in the spatial domain and in the

911
00:35:37,920 --> 00:35:42,240
frequency domain.

912
00:35:40,079 --> 00:35:44,800
In the top left is our original image, a

913
00:35:42,240 --> 00:35:47,040
closeup of an eye. Next to it in the top

914
00:35:44,800 --> 00:35:49,200
right, we see its two-dimensional forier

915
00:35:47,040 --> 00:35:51,599
transform which shows the frequency

916
00:35:49,200 --> 00:35:53,280
content of the image.

917
00:35:51,599 --> 00:35:55,040
On the second row, we have three

918
00:35:53,280 --> 00:35:58,160
filtered versions of the image in the

919
00:35:55,040 --> 00:36:00,400
spatial domain. Unsharp image produced

920
00:35:58,160 --> 00:36:02,560
by applying an unsharp filter which

921
00:36:00,400 --> 00:36:04,320
enhances edges and fine details by

922
00:36:02,560 --> 00:36:06,640
subtracting a blurred version of the

923
00:36:04,320 --> 00:36:09,280
image from the original.

924
00:36:06,640 --> 00:36:11,359
Gaussian blurred image generated using a

925
00:36:09,280 --> 00:36:13,599
Gaussian filter which smooths the image

926
00:36:11,359 --> 00:36:17,839
by reducing high frequency details

927
00:36:13,599 --> 00:36:20,720
resulting in a softer appearance.

928
00:36:17,839 --> 00:36:22,640
So filtered image created using the soil

929
00:36:20,720 --> 00:36:25,359
operator which emphasizes edges

930
00:36:22,640 --> 00:36:27,040
particularly strong intensity changes in

931
00:36:25,359 --> 00:36:28,880
the eye image. You can clearly see the

932
00:36:27,040 --> 00:36:31,119
contours of the iris, eyelid, and

933
00:36:28,880 --> 00:36:32,960
surrounding features.

934
00:36:31,119 --> 00:36:34,880
The third row shows the corresponding

935
00:36:32,960 --> 00:36:36,720
frequency domain representations for

936
00:36:34,880 --> 00:36:38,720
each filter.

937
00:36:36,720 --> 00:36:40,720
For the Gaussian blur, you can see that

938
00:36:38,720 --> 00:36:43,280
high frequency components are greatly

939
00:36:40,720 --> 00:36:45,280
reduced as indicated by the darker edges

940
00:36:43,280 --> 00:36:47,040
in the spectrum.

941
00:36:45,280 --> 00:36:48,960
For the unsharp filter, higher

942
00:36:47,040 --> 00:36:51,599
frequencies are more pronounced, showing

943
00:36:48,960 --> 00:36:53,599
stronger edge components.

944
00:36:51,599 --> 00:36:55,680
For the soil filter, the spectrum

945
00:36:53,599 --> 00:36:58,160
highlights frequencies associated with

946
00:36:55,680 --> 00:37:01,599
sharp transitions in the image, which is

947
00:36:58,160 --> 00:37:03,359
why it's effective for edge detection.

948
00:37:01,599 --> 00:37:05,280
By choosing different filters, we can

949
00:37:03,359 --> 00:37:07,280
highlight or suppress specific image

950
00:37:05,280 --> 00:37:09,760
features, whether we want to sharpen

951
00:37:07,280 --> 00:37:13,320
details, blur textures, or extract edges

952
00:37:09,760 --> 00:37:13,320
for further analysis.

953
00:37:15,040 --> 00:37:18,240
That brings us to the end of today's

954
00:37:16,560 --> 00:37:20,480
lecture.

955
00:37:18,240 --> 00:37:23,040
We've covered quite a lot from discrete

956
00:37:20,480 --> 00:37:25,280
convolution and zero padding to spectral

957
00:37:23,040 --> 00:37:27,280
analysis, bin refinement, and practical

958
00:37:25,280 --> 00:37:30,000
applications in two-dimensional image

959
00:37:27,280 --> 00:37:31,839
filtering. I hope you now have a clearer

960
00:37:30,000 --> 00:37:33,839
picture of how these concepts connect

961
00:37:31,839 --> 00:37:36,800
and how they are applied in real world

962
00:37:33,839 --> 00:37:38,560
signal and image processing.

963
00:37:36,800 --> 00:37:40,960
Take some time to review the examples

964
00:37:38,560 --> 00:37:42,960
and try the mat lab code on your own.

965
00:37:40,960 --> 00:37:45,680
Seeing the results firsthand will help

966
00:37:42,960 --> 00:37:47,440
solidify your understanding.

967
